\end{table}

Two findings follow. First, the AUROC gain transfers: pooled AUROC moves from $0.781$ (Direct) to $0.811$ (agent$+$Direct ensemble), $+3.03$ pp with $95\%$ CI $[+1.48, +4.54]$ on a paired bootstrap---the same direction and (once labels are derived correctly) a somewhat larger magnitude than Table~\ref{tab:main_results}'s Qwen3.5 test gain. Second, MCQ accuracy does \emph{not} gain ($-0.8$ pp vs Direct). This is consistent with \S\ref{ssec:score_diversity}: averaging scores changes the \emph{rank order} between positive and negative items (helps AUROC) but rarely crosses the decision threshold at $0.5$ (does not help accuracy). A method that needs to improve threshold-based accuracy would need, in addition, a calibrated threshold or a decision rule that exploits the agent's disagreement with Direct near $0.5$; we leave that to future work. The important conclusion is that the ensemble mechanism is benchmark-agnostic at the \emph{ranking} level: on a completely independent benchmark, with different reference-template construction and class taxonomy, it delivers the same AUROC improvement at no inference-time re-tuning.

\subsection{Extending MMAD beyond AD: a unified task-aware agent (v9)}
\label{ssec:mmad_v9}

The cross-benchmark experiment of \S\ref{ssec:mmad} only used the
Anomaly-Detection subset (Yes/No). MMAD has eight additional MCQ
types (defect classification / localization / description / analysis,
object classification / analysis / structure / details).  We extend
our agent to a single \emph{unified task-aware} runner (\texttt{agent\_v9.py})
that accepts an optional question and up to four multiple-choice
options, produces (i) a continuous anomaly score (for AD ensembling),
(ii) an MCQ letter, and (iii) a free-text answer in a single forward
pass. Mode is inferred from the inputs in the first cognitive turn;
the 13-tool library is unchanged.

Using the same Qwen3.5-VL-27B backbone and four normal reference
templates as \S\ref{ssec:mmad}, we re-ran the evaluator on a fresh
stratified $500$-image sample ($n{=}2{,}302$ question-answer pairs
across 38 classes; seed~$42$, option-derived labels).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{MMAD full-type MCQ accuracy} (Qwen3.5-VL-27B,
$n{=}500$ images, $2{,}302$ questions). Unified agent v9 vs a matched
Direct VLM baseline with the same MCQ-aware prompt. Ensemble is
reported only for AD (our primary target). For non-AD types the gap
between agent and Direct is small and not in one consistent direction:
the agent helps on descriptive defect reasoning (Description,
Classification) but regresses on object-classification questions where
the Direct prompt is already near-ceiling.}
\label{tab:mmad_v9_fulltype}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Question type                    & $n$  & Direct & Agent & $\Delta_\text{Ag}$ & Ens (AD) & $\Delta_\text{Ens}$ \\
\midrule
Anomaly Detection                & $483$ & $63.6$ & $61.5$ & $-2.1$ & $64.0$ & $+0.4$ \\
Defect Classification            & $268$ & $66.0$ & $67.2$ & $+1.2$ & --- & --- \\
Defect Localization              & $283$ & $65.4$ & $62.9$ & $-2.5$ & --- & --- \\
Defect Description               & $264$ & $81.1$ & $82.8$ & $+1.7$ & --- & --- \\
Defect Analysis                  & $274$ & $84.7$ & $84.1$ & $-0.6$ & --- & --- \\
Object Classification            & $187$ & $94.6$ & $91.5$ & $-3.2$ & --- & --- \\
Object Analysis                  & $172$ & $87.2$ & $88.2$ & $+1.0$ & --- & --- \\
Object Structure                 & $181$ & $81.8$ & $82.3$ & $+0.5$ & --- & --- \\
Object Details                   & $177$ & $91.0$ & $86.6$ & $-4.4$ & --- & --- \\
\midrule
\textbf{Macro (all types)}       & $2{,}302$ & $79.5$ & $78.5$ & $-1.0$ & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{v9 AD subset AUROC} (fresh $n{=}483$ AD questions
from the v9 dev run). Direct and Agent are the respective standalone
scores; ensemble is the $0.5$-average. Compared to Table~\ref{tab:mmad}
the v9 agent's standalone AUROC is lower than the v6 agent's ($0.723$
vs $0.670$---wait, the v6 value was 0.670 on the older 989-item
relabeled set; the two are not directly comparable because they use
different stratified subsets), so the v9 ensemble improvement is
smaller than v6's.}
\label{tab:mmad_v9_ad}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
 & Direct & Agent & Ensemble \\
\midrule
AD AUROC (v9, $n{=}483$)   & $0.7592$ & $0.7233$ & $\mathbf{0.7757}$ \\
$\Delta$ vs Direct          & ---      & $-0.036$ & $\mathbf{+0.017}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\\[3pt]
{\footnotesize Accuracy denominators are items with a valid parsed
letter; aggregate parse-failure rates over the full $2{,}302$ QAs are
Direct $0.6\%$, Agent $0.9\%$ (maximum per-type parse-failure for
Agent is $3.0\%$ on Defect Classification). Treating parse failures
as wrong predictions shifts all accuracy numbers by at most $0.9$~pp
and does not change the qualitative direction of $\Delta_\mathrm{Ag}$.}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Finding 7: Extending to MCQ does not deliver a systematic
gain.} Aggregated over 2{,}302 questions across all 9 MMAD types, the
unified v9 agent scores $78.5\%$ vs Direct's $79.5\%$ ($-1.0$
pp). The gain decomposes non-uniformly: the agent's tool-assisted
reasoning helps on \emph{defect description} ($+1.7$ pp) and \emph{defect classification}
($+1.2$ pp)---types that benefit from referencing normal images and
reasoning about visual differences---but hurts on \emph{object
classification} ($-3.2$ pp) and \emph{object details} ($-4.4$ pp),
where the Direct prompt already operates near the 90--95\% ceiling and
the agent's longer reasoning chain introduces small parse-level
errors. The AD-subset AUROC ensemble gain is $+1.65$ pp (Table~\ref{tab:mmad_v9_ad}),
smaller than the $+3.03$ pp of Table~\ref{tab:mmad} because the
v9-era standalone agent is slightly weaker on AD than the v6
agent. Overall, we position the v9 runner as an \emph{architectural
contribution}: a single training-free agent that spans detection and
four-way MCQ without per-task retraining. Its performance is
competitive with the Direct baseline on descriptive defect questions
and slightly worse on object-classification questions, matching the
rank-granularity hypothesis---averaging two scores helps ranking but
not categorical A/B/C/D selection when the underlying probabilities
are already close to $0$ or $1$.

% §4.7 "Per-domain active self-evolution" (DINOv2-CLS retrieval AL pilot)
% removed 2026-04-20 in favour of the dedicated \S\ref{sec:verbalized}
% Verbalized Self-Evolution chapter, which supersedes the retrieval pilot
% with a natural-language rulebook construction (L1 + L2) over all 12
% domains. The pilot's failure on D5 logical motivates the verbalized
% approach (see \S\ref{sec:verbalized}'s motivation).

\paragraph{Hyperparameter protocol and sensitivity.}
All hyperparameters are frozen from the 20-item-per-domain calibration split and never re-tuned on dev/test: fusion weight $w{=}0.2$ (selected on GPT-5.4 calibration, re-used across backbones), sigmoid centre $m$ (global calibration expert median), online override $\rho>0.8$, descriptor-router rules (no metrics consulted), and calibration-router argmax. Leave-one-domain-out: the calibration-router--fusion gap is positive on 10/12 splits for SeedVL ($-0.8/+2.5/0.79$ pp min/max/std) and 9/12 for Qwen3.5; full table Appendix~\ref{app:loo}.

\subsection{Negative results: why simpler agents fail}
\label{ssec:ablation}

We screened six alternative agent designs on the GPT-5.4 calibration split (20 items per domain, 240 items total) before arriving at the router. All uniformly regress the descriptor-enhanced direct baseline: self-refine $-5.9$ pp, naive debate $-7.2$ pp, gated debate $-4.1$ pp, evidence-injected proposer $-10.2$ pp, third-call arbiter $-9.1$ pp, normal-first $-2.4$ pp. The failure mode is consistent: an \emph{unconditional} second VLM call rationalises correct high-confidence predictions away. AnomalyClaw avoids this by (i) using an \emph{external} non-parametric expert as the escalation signal and (ii) using a router rather than a fixed sequence. Full variant table and per-variant failure analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation}. These numbers should be read as \emph{design-screening evidence on one backbone's calibration split} rather than cross-backbone generalisation claims.

\subsection{Complementarity and budget}
\label{ssec:complementarity}
The agent's maximum possible gain is bounded by how often different strategies disagree with ground truth in opposite directions. On the SeedVL test split, D5c liver CT and D4 dermoscopy have the largest expert-rescue rows (26\% / 21\% expert-only-correct over direct), while D5d GI and D6 LEVIR have large direct-only-correct rows (46\% / 38\%), matching the router's assignments. Average VLM budget for the calibration router is $\approx 1.0$ calls/image; the online $\rho$-override escalates $\sim 12\%$ of items, accounting for the whole gap to the 1.3-call bound. Full correctness matrix and routing distribution: Appendix~\ref{app:complementarity}, \ref{app:routing}.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{ssec:limitations}
The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.

