paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:4:Table~\ref{tab:benchmark_detail} provides per-domain details for CrossDomainVAD-11. All splits, item IDs, reference mappings, and domain descriptors will be released as a JSON specification file.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:8:\caption{CrossDomainVAD-11 per-domain specification.}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:16:D01 & Industrial & MVTec-AD & Surface defect, damage, contamination & 10 & CC BY-NC-SA \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:18:D03 & Infrastructure & SDNET2018 & Cracks, spalling, structural damage & 10 & Public \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:19:D04 & Dermoscopy & DermaMNIST & Malignant melanoma & 10 & CC BY \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:20:D05 & Brain MRI & BraTS2021 & Glioma, mass effect, oedema & 10 & CC BY \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:24:D09 & Road Obstacle & RA21+BDD & Unexpected object on roadway & 2 & Research \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:25:D10 & Logical & MVTec-LOCO & Wrong count/arrangement of components & 10 & CC BY-NC-SA \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:26:D11 & VisA & VisA & Surface defect, chips, scratches & 10 & CC BY-NC-SA \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:38:The descriptor $d$ is a single sentence inserted into every VLM prompt. The eleven descriptors used in CrossDomainVAD-11 are:
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:40:    \item \textbf{D01 Industrial (MVTec-AD):} Normal = a manufactured object matching the reference in geometry, texture, and colour. Anomaly = surface defect, damage, contamination, or missing/extra component.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:42:    \item \textbf{D03 Infrastructure (SDNET2018):} Normal = intact concrete surface. Anomaly = crack, spalling, or structural damage.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:43:    \item \textbf{D04 Dermoscopy (DermaMNIST):} Normal = benign melanocytic skin lesion. Anomaly = malignant melanoma.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:44:    \item \textbf{D05 Brain MRI (BraTS2021):} Normal = healthy brain MRI slice. Anomaly = glioma, mass effect, or peritumoural oedema.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:45:    \item \textbf{D06 Liver CT (BMAD-Liver):} Normal = healthy liver CT slice. Anomaly = focal lesion, tumour, or cyst.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:48:    \item \textbf{D09 Road Obstacle (RA21+BDD):} Normal = empty driveable roadway (possibly with expected vehicles and markings). Anomaly = unexpected object on the roadway.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:49:    \item \textbf{D10 Logical (MVTec-LOCO):} Normal = correct count and spatial arrangement of components. Anomaly = wrong count, wrong position, or wrong component type.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:50:    \item \textbf{D11 VisA (VisA):} Normal = a manufactured object matching the reference. Anomaly = surface defect, chips, scratches, or missing component.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:110:We benchmarked three expert models on the calibration split (Table~\ref{tab:expert_appendix}). SubspaceAD dominates overall, reaching 1.000 AUROC on D11 VisA calibration where all VLMs score $\leq 0.85$. AnomalyVFM (zero-shot, DINOv2 + LoRA) is uniformly weaker (macro 0.599) and was excluded from AnomalyClaw. DINOv2-PatchNN complements SubspaceAD on D03 infrastructure (0.80 vs 0.50). The oracle that selects the best expert per domain reaches macro 0.898, indicating 7 pp headroom for multi-expert routing as future work.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:121:DINOv2-PatchNN & 0.640 & D09 road (1.00) \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:122:\textbf{SubspaceAD} & \textbf{0.756} & D11 VisA (1.00) \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:150:D05 Brain MRI       & 0.915 & 0.934 & $+$1.9  & 0.736 & 0.864 & \textbf{$+$12.8} & 0.866 & 0.849 & $-$1.8 \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:155:D10 Logical         & 0.660 & 0.683 & $+$2.3  & 0.678 & 0.651 & $-$2.7 & 0.641 & 0.676 & $+$3.5 \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:156:D11 VisA            & 0.873 & 0.878 & $+$0.5  & 0.805 & 0.878 & $+$7.3 & 0.776 & 0.800 & $+$2.4 \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:191:D05 Brain MRI     & 0.52 & 0.89 & \textbf{0.88} & 0.85 \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:196:D10 Logical       & 0.62 & 0.66 & 0.66 & \textbf{0.71} \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:197:D11 VisA          & 0.60 & \textbf{0.91} & 0.83 & \textbf{0.91} \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:290:\textbf{Reasoning (verbatim from Qwen3.5):} ``\emph{SubspaceAD is the designated expert for liver CT texture anomalies, and the interpret strategy provides a critical safety net to verify subtle focal lesions that the VLM may miss. The hotspot cropper makes the second-call evidence explicit when the expert flags a concentrated abnormality.}''\\[2pt]
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:294:\paragraph{Per-item agent execution (D1 Industrial, item \texttt{D1\_0063}, SeedVL).}
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:296:\textbf{Phase 1 (Plan lookup).} Cached plan for D1 = (\texttt{strategy:fusion}, \texttt{expert:subspacead}, tools \{\texttt{domain\_descriptor}, \texttt{hotspot\_cropper}\}).\\[2pt]
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:308:We prototyped a fourth route, \emph{Component Enumeration} (Route C), for logical anomalies (D10 MVTec-LOCO) where the anomaly is defined by wrong count or wrong spatial arrangement of components rather than by a texture defect. Route C triggers when $\rho{>}1.2$ and $\kappa{<}1.10$ (strong but \emph{dispersed} expert signal). The action is a second VLM call with a specialised enumerate prompt that asks the model to list components in the query versus the references and report count differences.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:310:On D10 calibration (20 items, 4 reference images per query), Route C reaches 60\% accuracy versus v0's 55\%. The gain is modest for two reasons: (i) VLMs struggle to count small similar objects reliably, and (ii) the 20-item calibration split yields noisy threshold estimates. Route C is \emph{not} part of the main-table AnomalyClaw variant reported in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: the reported numbers use only Routes A/B/D. We include Route C here as a concrete direction for future work and flag it as a prototype rather than a validated component of the agent.
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:343:logical (D9 MVTec-LOCO) & fusion & component structure visible to patch expert; VLM rules out semantic look-alikes \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:345:medical\_mixed / liver\_ct / brain\_mri & fusion & SubspaceAD complements VLM on non-trivial intensity shifts \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:347:remote-sensing change (D6 LEVIR) & direct & change semantics require descriptor-level task anchoring \\
paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:348:road (D8, D9 RA21+BDD) & direct & semantic obstacle recognition dominates; expert is a distractor \\
paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:4:We introduced \textbf{AnomalyClaw}, a training-free visual anomaly detection framework whose headline method is score-diverse VLM-agent ensembling: a simple arithmetic average of a Direct VLM score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score. On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} ($1{,}418$ test items, 12 domains) the ensemble reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating descriptor-only Direct by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). On GPT-5.4 it reaches $0.864$, edging the SubspaceAD-fusion baseline by $+0.9$ pp while using no external expert model. We localise the mechanism to \emph{score-rank granularity}: Direct emits $11$ unique scores on the test set, the agent emits $49$; averaging breaks ties inside Direct's coarse bimodal clusters with the agent's finer rank information. Controlled rank-preserving transformations confirm this: $0\%$ middle-mass with $49$ unique values still delivers $+4.68$ pp, whereas $0\%$ middle-mass with only $2$ unique values delivers $+2.31$ pp.
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:9:    \caption{\textbf{AnomalyClaw on CrossDomainVAD-11.}
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:10:    \textbf{(a)} No single strategy wins uniformly across domains: the best direct/fusion/debate/interpret cell differs by family (industrial, medical, logical, GI, change, road). \textbf{(b)} A calibration-driven router matches or beats the strongest single strategy on all three backbones and captures 40--60\% of the oracle gap; a zero-data descriptor router recovers much of the gain for free. \textbf{(c)} Per-domain strategy assignment is well-aligned with the oracle (9--10/12 agreement across backbones).}
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:14:Two observations drive this work. First, a cross-domain VAD deployment must handle textural industrial defects, semantic road obstacles, brain-MRI lesions, and infrastructure cracks with the same pipeline. The same fixed recipe---no matter how clever---cannot be optimal on all of them simultaneously: we find that on our 12-domain test split, the argmax strategy varies by domain family and gains of up to $+3$ pp macro AUROC are available simply by switching strategy per domain (Figure~\ref{fig:intuition}a). Second, recent ``agentic VAD'' systems~\citep{zhang2025agentiad,chen2026eagle,wei2025autoiad} bundle three decisions---what the VLM attends to, which expert provides evidence, and how to aggregate---into a single monolithic pipeline that is hard to ablate and hard to compare against simpler baselines.
paper/sections/1_introduction.tex:32:    \item \textbf{Benchmark and framework release.} We release \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domain codes, $1{,}418$ test items, calibration/dev/test splits), the Tool/Expert/Strategy registry, the calibration-argmax router, the v8 refutation implementation with full trace logging, a 13-tool single-tool audit on Qwen3.5 dev, six failed-variant ablations, and stratified paired bootstrap confidence intervals for every headline comparison.
paper/sections/0_abstract.tex:3:\textbf{Main finding.} On \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11} (12 domains, $1{,}418$ test items), a simple arithmetic ensemble of the VLM's Direct score and a tool-using ReAct agent's score reaches macro AUROC $0.8136$ on Qwen3.5-VL-27B, beating the descriptor-only Direct VLM baseline $0.7684$ by $+4.53$ pp (stratified paired bootstrap 95\% CI $[+2.82, +6.31]$, $P(\Delta\!>\!0){=}1.000$). The ensemble recipe is $s_{\mathrm{final}} = 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{Direct}} + 0.5\!\cdot\!s_{\mathrm{agent}}$, \emph{frozen} on dev before any test query is seen. The recipe beats the stronger SubspaceAD \emph{fusion} baseline by $+0.9$ pp on GPT-5.4 and matches it on Qwen3.5, using only VLM outputs, no additional expert model.

