review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:409:We evaluate on \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11}, spanning twelve domain codes grouped into 11 reported families: D1 MVTec-AD industrial, D2 GoodsAD retail, D3 SDNET2018 concrete infrastructure (dev only), D4 DermaMNIST dermoscopy, D5 BraTS2021 brain MRI, D5b BMAD brain MRI, D5c BMAD liver CT, D5d HyperKvasir GI endoscopy variant, D6 LEVIR-CD+ remote-sensing change, D7 HyperKvasir GI endoscopy, D8 RA21+BDD road, D9 MVTec-LOCO logical, D10 VisA industrial. Each domain contributes 20 calibration / 40 dev / 120 test items (D8: 98 test). All items are reference-based, paired with 1--10 normal references. Total test items $n{=}1418$ (fusion strategies drop D8 items lacking SubspaceAD support to $n{=}1298$ and fall back to direct on the rest). The full per-domain specification is in Appendix~\ref{app:benchmark}.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:478:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:562:The agent's maximum possible gain is bounded by how often different strategies disagree with ground truth in opposite directions. On the SeedVL test split, D5c liver CT and D4 dermoscopy have the largest expert-rescue rows (26\% / 21\% expert-only-correct over direct), while D5d GI and D6 LEVIR have large direct-only-correct rows (46\% / 38\%), matching the router's assignments. Average VLM budget for the calibration router is $\approx 1.0$ calls/image; the online $\rho$-override escalates $\sim 12\%$ of items, accounting for the whole gap to the 1.3-call bound. Full correctness matrix and routing distribution: Appendix~\ref{app:complementarity}, \ref{app:routing}.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:566:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:1699:**Win items** (n=43): domains={'D9': 7, 'D5c': 7, 'D1': 5, 'D6': 5, 'D2': 4, 'D10': 4}; label_pos_rate=[(0, 26), (1, 17)]; rank(hi/mid/lo)=14/14/15; direct_mid(uncertain)=0; direct_conf(extreme)=43; tool_used=18/43
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:1701:**Loss items** (n=71): domains={'D6': 18, 'D9': 11, 'D5': 9, 'D2': 7, 'D8': 6, 'D5d': 6}; label_pos_rate=[(1, 44), (0, 27)]; rank(hi/mid/lo)=19/38/14; direct_mid(uncertain)=0; direct_conf(extreme)=71; tool_used=53/71
review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:1820:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:77:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
