review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1354:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:80:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs the stronger SubspaceAD-fusion baseline), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation; $\pm 0$ vs fusion, i.e.\ matches fusion with no expert model required), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp over Direct, $+0.14$ vs fusion, not significant). The scientific content of the comparison is ``VLM-only ensemble edges fusion on GPT-5.4 and matches fusion on Qwen3.5, without the SubspaceAD expert''---the right target to foreground because SubspaceAD is already a strong training-free baseline (\S\ref{ssec:setup}). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by $+3$--$6$ pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1400:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:203:The agent's maximum possible gain is bounded by how often different strategies disagree with ground truth in opposite directions. On the SeedVL test split, D5c liver CT and D4 dermoscopy have the largest expert-rescue rows (26\% / 21\% expert-only-correct over direct), while D5d GI and D6 LEVIR have large direct-only-correct rows (46\% / 38\%), matching the router's assignments. Average VLM budget for the calibration router is $\approx 1.0$ calls/image; the online $\rho$-override escalates $\sim 12\%$ of items, accounting for the whole gap to the 1.3-call bound. Full correctness matrix and routing distribution: Appendix~\ref{app:complementarity}, \ref{app:routing}.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1401:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:207:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1422:paper/sections/2_related_work.tex:11:A recent wave of work adds multi-turn reasoning, tool use, retrieval, and memory on top of a base VLM. AgentIAD~\citep{zhang2025agentiad} orchestrates a VLM with a patch expert via a tool interface; EAGLE~\citep{chen2026eagle} and similar systems introduce multi-role debate between VLM-based agents; AutoIAD~\citep{wei2025autoiad} adds a routing controller that selects methods per image. These systems report gains over single-pass baselines, but three structural issues make the gains hard to attribute: (a) they bundle \emph{what to look at} (tools), \emph{who provides evidence} (expert choice), and \emph{how to aggregate} (strategy) into a single monolithic pipeline; (b) they typically compare to a generic-descriptor VLM baseline that we show is badly under-calibrated; (c) extra VLM calls, retrieval indices, per-domain calibration, and multi-turn exchange all confound the ablation. AnomalyClaw makes three deliberate choices. First, the framework \emph{decouples} the three decisions (Tool, Expert, Strategy) so that each can be swapped independently and reported separately. Second, our baselines include both the descriptor-enhanced single-pass call (a strong baseline most prior agentic work did not report) and a zero-cost score-fusion baseline that already matches or exceeds every single agent strategy on our benchmark. Third, our router picks one of four strategies via calibration-argmax or descriptor rules---it does not invent new inference on top of the chosen strategy---and the ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation} documents six alternative agent designs that all \emph{hurt} the stronger baseline on GPT-5.4 calibration.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r1_raw.out:1505:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:287:\paragraph{Autonomous router plan (D5c BMAD-Liver, Qwen3.5).}
