review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:1455:refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3816:A publishable angle is possible if the paper is reframed honestly: “tool-augmented VLM agents are brittle; simple fusion is a strong baseline; agents help only under identifiable reliability conditions.” For a positive agent paper, you need a frozen dev-selected method, a clean held-out test, no contaminated retrieval bank, and evidence that tool use causally improves rank ordering beyond Direct and fixed fusion.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:1458:refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3828:5. Create a dev-only routing policy from non-label features available at inference: domain descriptor, first-turn uncertainty, expert raw/rank, and tool observation metadata. Freeze it, then evaluate once on held-out test as a pure agent controller.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:1464:refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3882:A publishable angle is possible if the paper is reframed honestly: “tool-augmented VLM agents are brittle; simple fusion is a strong baseline; agents help only under identifiable reliability conditions.” For a positive agent paper, you need a frozen dev-selected method, a clean held-out test, no contaminated retrieval bank, and evidence that tool use causally improves rank ordering beyond Direct and fixed fusion.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:1467:refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18.md:3894:5. Create a dev-only routing policy from non-label features available at inference: domain descriptor, first-turn uncertainty, expert raw/rank, and tool observation metadata. Freeze it, then evaluate once on held-out test as a pure agent controller.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:1592:review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:478:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
