review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:2106:review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2351:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:77:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:2142:review-stage/codex_review_r2_raw.out:2687:paper/sections/2_related_work.tex:11:A recent wave of work adds multi-turn reasoning, tool use, retrieval, and memory on top of a base VLM. AgentIAD~\citep{zhang2025agentiad} orchestrates a VLM with a patch expert via a tool interface; EAGLE~\citep{chen2026eagle} and similar systems introduce multi-role debate between VLM-based agents; AutoIAD~\citep{wei2025autoiad} adds a routing controller that selects methods per image. These systems report gains over single-pass baselines, but three structural issues make the gains hard to attribute: (a) they bundle \emph{what to look at} (tools), \emph{who provides evidence} (expert choice), and \emph{how to aggregate} (strategy) into a single monolithic pipeline; (b) they typically compare to a generic-descriptor VLM baseline that we show is badly under-calibrated; (c) extra VLM calls, retrieval indices, per-domain calibration, and multi-turn exchange all confound the ablation. AnomalyClaw makes three deliberate choices. First, the framework \emph{decouples} the three decisions (Tool, Expert, Strategy) so that each can be swapped independently and reported separately. Second, our baselines include both the descriptor-enhanced single-pass call (a strong baseline most prior agentic work did not report) and a zero-cost score-fusion baseline that already matches or exceeds every single agent strategy on our benchmark. Third, our router picks one of four strategies via calibration-argmax or descriptor rules---it does not invent new inference on top of the chosen strategy---and the ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation} documents six alternative agent designs that all \emph{hurt} the stronger baseline on GPT-5.4 calibration.
