review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:4722:review-stage/codex_review_r1_raw.out:2841:    77	Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5278:paper/sections/2_related_work.tex:11:A recent wave of work adds multi-turn reasoning, tool use, retrieval, and memory on top of a base VLM. AgentIAD~\citep{zhang2025agentiad} orchestrates a VLM with a patch expert via a tool interface; EAGLE~\citep{chen2026eagle} and similar systems introduce multi-role debate between VLM-based agents; AutoIAD~\citep{wei2025autoiad} adds a routing controller that selects methods per image. These systems report gains over single-pass baselines, but three structural issues make the gains hard to attribute: (a) they bundle \emph{what to look at} (tools), \emph{who provides evidence} (expert choice), and \emph{how to aggregate} (strategy) into a single monolithic pipeline; (b) they typically compare to a generic-descriptor VLM baseline that we show is badly under-calibrated; (c) extra VLM calls, retrieval indices, per-domain calibration, and multi-turn exchange all confound the ablation. AnomalyClaw makes three deliberate choices. First, the framework \emph{decouples} the three decisions (Tool, Expert, Strategy) so that each can be swapped independently and reported separately. Second, our baselines include both the descriptor-enhanced single-pass call (a strong baseline most prior agentic work did not report) and a zero-cost score-fusion baseline that already matches or exceeds every single agent strategy on our benchmark. Third, our router picks one of four strategies via calibration-argmax or descriptor rules---it does not invent new inference on top of the chosen strategy---and the ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation} documents six alternative agent designs that all \emph{hurt} the stronger baseline on GPT-5.4 calibration.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5306:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:287:\paragraph{Autonomous router plan (D5c BMAD-Liver, Qwen3.5).}
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5320:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:343:logical (D9 MVTec-LOCO) & fusion & component structure visible to patch expert; VLM rules out semantic look-alikes \\
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5421:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:8:We evaluate on \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11}, spanning twelve domain codes grouped into 11 reported families: D1 MVTec-AD industrial, D2 GoodsAD retail, D3 SDNET2018 concrete infrastructure (dev only), D4 DermaMNIST dermoscopy, D5 BraTS2021 brain MRI, D5b BMAD brain MRI, D5c BMAD liver CT, D5d HyperKvasir GI endoscopy variant, D6 LEVIR-CD+ remote-sensing change, D7 HyperKvasir GI endoscopy, D8 RA21+BDD road, D9 MVTec-LOCO logical, D10 VisA industrial. Each domain contributes 20 calibration / 40 dev / 120 test items (D8: 98 test). All items are reference-based, paired with 1--10 normal references. Total test items $n{=}1418$ (fusion strategies drop D8 items lacking SubspaceAD support to $n{=}1298$ and fall back to direct on the rest). The full per-domain specification is in Appendix~\ref{app:benchmark}.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5427:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:77:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5446:paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:172:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5447:paper/sections/2_related_work.tex:11:A recent wave of work adds multi-turn reasoning, tool use, retrieval, and memory on top of a base VLM. AgentIAD~\citep{zhang2025agentiad} orchestrates a VLM with a patch expert via a tool interface; EAGLE~\citep{chen2026eagle} and similar systems introduce multi-role debate between VLM-based agents; AutoIAD~\citep{wei2025autoiad} adds a routing controller that selects methods per image. These systems report gains over single-pass baselines, but three structural issues make the gains hard to attribute: (a) they bundle \emph{what to look at} (tools), \emph{who provides evidence} (expert choice), and \emph{how to aggregate} (strategy) into a single monolithic pipeline; (b) they typically compare to a generic-descriptor VLM baseline that we show is badly under-calibrated; (c) extra VLM calls, retrieval indices, per-domain calibration, and multi-turn exchange all confound the ablation. AnomalyClaw makes three deliberate choices. First, the framework \emph{decouples} the three decisions (Tool, Expert, Strategy) so that each can be swapped independently and reported separately. Second, our baselines include both the descriptor-enhanced single-pass call (a strong baseline most prior agentic work did not report) and a zero-cost score-fusion baseline that already matches or exceeds every single agent strategy on our benchmark. Third, our router picks one of four strategies via calibration-argmax or descriptor rules---it does not invent new inference on top of the chosen strategy---and the ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation} documents six alternative agent designs that all \emph{hurt} the stronger baseline on GPT-5.4 calibration.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5450:paper/sections/5_conclusion.tex:7:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle upper bound leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom. Routing thresholds and rules are frozen from 20 calibration items per domain. The Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) achieves 60\% on D9 calibration and is not in the reported router. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5471:paper/sections/A_appendix.tex:343:logical (D9 MVTec-LOCO) & fusion & component structure visible to patch expert; VLM rules out semantic look-alikes \\
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5544:    11	A recent wave of work adds multi-turn reasoning, tool use, retrieval, and memory on top of a base VLM. AgentIAD~\citep{zhang2025agentiad} orchestrates a VLM with a patch expert via a tool interface; EAGLE~\citep{chen2026eagle} and similar systems introduce multi-role debate between VLM-based agents; AutoIAD~\citep{wei2025autoiad} adds a routing controller that selects methods per image. These systems report gains over single-pass baselines, but three structural issues make the gains hard to attribute: (a) they bundle \emph{what to look at} (tools), \emph{who provides evidence} (expert choice), and \emph{how to aggregate} (strategy) into a single monolithic pipeline; (b) they typically compare to a generic-descriptor VLM baseline that we show is badly under-calibrated; (c) extra VLM calls, retrieval indices, per-domain calibration, and multi-turn exchange all confound the ablation. AnomalyClaw makes three deliberate choices. First, the framework \emph{decouples} the three decisions (Tool, Expert, Strategy) so that each can be swapped independently and reported separately. Second, our baselines include both the descriptor-enhanced single-pass call (a strong baseline most prior agentic work did not report) and a zero-cost score-fusion baseline that already matches or exceeds every single agent strategy on our benchmark. Third, our router picks one of four strategies via calibration-argmax or descriptor rules---it does not invent new inference on top of the chosen strategy---and the ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation} documents six alternative agent designs that all \emph{hurt} the stronger baseline on GPT-5.4 calibration.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5683:     7	The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle upper bound leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom. Routing thresholds and rules are frozen from 20 calibration items per domain. The Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) achieves 60\% on D9 calibration and is not in the reported router. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only.
review-stage/codex_review_r3_raw.out:5691:    77	Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs Fusion), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation), and 0.809 on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp, not significant). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by +3--6 pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
