review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:770:./paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:8:We evaluate on \textbf{CrossDomainVAD-11}, spanning twelve domain codes grouped into 11 reported families: D1 MVTec-AD industrial, D2 GoodsAD retail, D3 SDNET2018 concrete infrastructure (dev only), D4 DermaMNIST dermoscopy, D5 BraTS2021 brain MRI, D5b BMAD brain MRI, D5c BMAD liver CT, D5d HyperKvasir GI endoscopy variant, D6 LEVIR-CD+ remote-sensing change, D7 HyperKvasir GI endoscopy, D8 RA21+BDD road, D9 MVTec-LOCO logical, D10 VisA industrial. Each domain contributes 20 calibration / 40 dev / 120 test items (D8: 98 test). All items are reference-based, paired with 1--10 normal references. Total test items $n{=}1418$ (fusion strategies drop D8 items lacking SubspaceAD support to $n{=}1298$ and fall back to direct on the rest). The full per-domain specification is in Appendix~\ref{app:benchmark}.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:786:./paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:80:Our \textbf{AnomalyClaw v6 agent} is a per-item autonomous ReAct loop: on each image the VLM sees a one-sentence domain description, four normal reference images, a 13-tool catalog, and a turn budget of $K{=}5$. Each turn it emits a JSON action (either a tool call with arguments, or a final anomaly score). No strategy or tool choice is hardcoded per domain. The 13 tools span five tiers: expert probes (four cached AD models), visual inspection (hotspot-crop, zoom-bbox, patch-grid, image-diff, rotate-align, side-by-side), reference understanding (profiler, retriever), structural (component-counter, segment-and-count, texture-FFT), and semantic knowledge lookup. Of the 13 tools, two make additional VLM or LLM calls inside the tool body (\texttt{tool\_reference\_profiler} runs one extra VLM call to summarise the reference-pool commonalities, \texttt{tool\_domain\_knowledge} routes a text question to an LLM); we account for these additional calls in the cost reporting of Appendix~\ref{app:cost} rather than classifying tools into ``zero-call'' vs ``call''. Agent alone already matches or beats Direct on GPT-5.4 (+1.1 pp) and Qwen3.5 ($+0.3$ pp, effectively tied at fair comparison) and SeedVL is slightly worse ($-1.7$ pp). Composing the agent's score with Direct VLM via a simple average ($\alpha{=}0.5$) yields the headline number in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}: \textbf{0.864} on GPT-5.4 ($+1.74$ pp vs Direct, $+0.87$ vs the stronger SubspaceAD-fusion baseline), \textbf{0.814} on Qwen3.5 ($+4.53$ pp vs Direct, $p{=}0.0005$ stratified paired permutation; $\pm 0$ vs fusion, i.e.\ matches fusion with no expert model required), and \textbf{0.809} on SeedVL ($+0.93$ pp over Direct, $95\%$ CI $[-0.32, +2.25]$, $P(\Delta{>}0){=}0.927$; positive in mean but \emph{not significant} at $95\%$, consistent with SeedVL being the weakest backbone in our pool). The scientific content of the comparison is ``VLM-only ensemble edges fusion on GPT-5.4 and matches fusion on Qwen3.5, without the SubspaceAD expert''---the right target to foreground because SubspaceAD is already a strong training-free baseline (\S\ref{ssec:setup}). The ensemble works because the agent's errors and Direct's errors are complementary on the strongest backbone: on GPT-5.4 the agent wins on D1/D5c/D9/D8 by $+3$--$6$ pp while Direct is stronger on D5d/D6 change/medical domains, and averaging captures both. Per-domain breakdown and per-tool usage statistics are in Appendix~\ref{app:perdomain}. The v6 code path is \texttt{agent\_v6.py} (core ReAct loop) plus \texttt{agent\_v6\_5.py} / \texttt{agent\_v6\_6.py} (variant runners) and \texttt{compose\_ensemble.py} (offline score composition).
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:829:./paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:288:\label{ssec:active_learning}
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:840:./paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:362:The agent's maximum possible gain is bounded by how often different strategies disagree with ground truth in opposite directions. On the SeedVL test split, D5c liver CT and D4 dermoscopy have the largest expert-rescue rows (26\% / 21\% expert-only-correct over direct), while D5d GI and D6 LEVIR have large direct-only-correct rows (46\% / 38\%), matching the router's assignments. Average VLM budget for the calibration router is $\approx 1.0$ calls/image; the online $\rho$-override escalates $\sim 12\%$ of items, accounting for the whole gap to the 1.3-call bound. Full correctness matrix and routing distribution: Appendix~\ref{app:complementarity}, \ref{app:routing}.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:841:./paper/sections/4_experiments.tex:366:The calibration router only beats fusion significantly on SeedVL; on GPT-5.4 it trails fusion by 0.5 pp (not significant) and on Qwen3.5 it matches fusion. The oracle leaves 1.3--1.9 pp further headroom, suggesting a VLM-driven router reading image-level features may close more of the gap. Routing is calibrated from 20 items per domain; we evaluate a single expert backbone (DINOv2-giant) and a single frontier non-English VLM (SeedVL); the Route-C component-enumeration prototype (Appendix~\ref{app:routec}) remains at 60\% calibration accuracy on D9 and is not in the reported variant. The failed-variant ablation is GPT-5.4 calibration only. The online expert-signal override threshold ($\rho > 0.8$) is currently calibrated against patch-$k$-NN's normalised score; on industrial domains where SubspaceAD's raw distance is large, the override fires on essentially every \emph{normal}-predicted item and effectively reduces the strategy choice to ``fusion or interpret'' on those domains; a backbone-aware $\rho$ rescaling is left to future work.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:849:./AnomaClaw/LIT_SURVEY.md:32:**Key gap:** **Zero** papers on active learning specifically designed for MVTec AD / VisA image-level benchmarks at top venues.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:850:./AnomaClaw/LIT_SURVEY.md:53:- **No paper** designs query strategies using agent reasoning traces (tool call patterns, reflection outputs, retrieval similarity scores) as uncertainty signals for active learning.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:854:./AnomaClaw/LIT_SURVEY.md:76:The closest work to our direction is AgentIAD, which adds tool-use to a VLM but remains fundamentally a structured CoT system (no state, no learning from feedback). NearCAIPI (ECCV 2024 Workshop) is the only work combining active learning with visual IAD, but it's unpublished at top venues and uses a simple uncertainty-based query strategy without agent signals.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:855:./AnomaClaw/LIT_SURVEY.md:78:**The intersection of (1) full agent reasoning loop + (2) active learning + (3) non-parametric memory update is completely unoccupied.**
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:905:./AnomaClaw/IDEA_REPORT.md:88:- **Expected outcome**: Diversity strategy > uncertainty ≈ random at k=5,10,15 budget; +3-8% AUROC over static 5-shot baseline
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:906:./AnomaClaw/IDEA_REPORT.md:109:- **Hypothesis**: Structural features of the Skeptic-Verifier's multi-round reasoning trace (debate length, consensus speed, semantic hedging tokens) are better calibrated uncertainty proxies than softmax confidence for IAD queries, enabling a more efficient active learning query strategy.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:908:./AnomaClaw/IDEA_REPORT.md:163:- **Hypothesis**: Establishing a standardized active learning evaluation protocol (fixed budget sizes, oracle simulation, evaluation metrics) reveals significant performance differences between query strategies and enables reproducible AL research in visual IAD.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:916:./RESUME.md:4:**Status**: v9 unified agent + MMAD full-type eval + active learning
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:964:./paper/PAPER_IMPROVEMENT_LOG.md:54:  Harmonized the benchmark description, corrected the domain-win count, corrected the D9 family-analysis number, softened causal language, converted AUROC deltas to points, added a visible Pareto placeholder, and replaced impossible failure-analysis counts with a verifiable summary.
review-stage/codex_v9_review_r2_raw.out:984:./AnomaClaw/refine-logs/round-0-initial-proposal.md:17:- **Better prompts**: Our domain-specific prompts (D5 medical) improved Spe from 10%→40% but AUROC stayed at 0.47. Prompts can't inject domain expertise the VLM doesn't have.
