 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	\section{Verbalized Self-Evolution}
     2	\label{sec:verbalized}
     3	
     4	Passive v9 (\S\ref{sec:experiments}) re-reads the normal reference images
     5	on every test query but never \emph{accumulates} anything from them:
     6	the same ref set produces the same ad-hoc reasoning trace a thousand
     7	times. The retrieval-based active-learning pilot we originally
     8	attempted (pre-review appendix, cf.\ \citet{al_pilot_appendix})
     9	partially addressed this by injecting a DINOv2-CLS-retrieved neighbour
    10	prior, but was bottlenecked by what visual-similarity embeddings can
    11	express: on \emph{logical} anomalies (wrong count, misordered
    12	components) no DINOv2-CLS neighbour encodes the defining predicate,
    13	and the pilot indeed regressed $-4.0$ pp on D5 MVTec-LOCO.
    14	
    15	This section introduces a \textbf{verbalized self-evolution} protocol
    16	that learns a persistent, natural-language \emph{rulebook} per domain
    17	(or per class, where applicable) and injects it into the agent's
    18	turn-$1$ system prompt at test time. Because rules are natural
    19	language, they can capture predicates the visual embedding cannot
    20	(``count the legs'', ``screws should be aligned''), and because the
    21	rulebook is produced \emph{once} per domain, its cost amortises across
    22	all test queries in that domain.
    23	
    24	\subsection{Three learning regimes}
    25	\label{ssec:verb_regimes}
    26	
    27	We frame this as a ladder of three learning regimes; the choice is
    28	about \emph{how much supervision the system touches}, not whether a
    29	rulebook is used at inference time:
    30	
    31	\begin{description}
    32	\item[\textbf{Passive v9}] — training-free. No rulebook; refs are read
    33	  per-query as in \S\ref{ssec:main_results}. This is the baseline.
    34	\item[\textbf{L1 — ref-only rulebook} (self-supervised, $0$ oracle)]
    35	  — reflect on the normal reference pool and emit a persistent
    36	  rulebook containing a \emph{normal signature} and a set of
    37	  \emph{hypothesized anomaly modes}. Uses zero oracle labels. The
    38	  rulebook \emph{is} a learned artifact; we therefore do not call L1
    39	  training-free, despite it touching no new labels.
    40	\item[\textbf{L2 — active verbalized rulebook} (semi-supervised,
    41	  $K{=}10$ oracle per domain)] — run passive v9 on dev, pick the $K$
    42	  highest-uncertainty dev items ($|s{-}0.5|$ smallest), obtain their
    43	  ground-truth labels from the dev manifest, and reflect each
    44	  (image, agent trajectory, label) tuple into a \emph{corrective
    45	  rule}. Only dev items are revealed; test labels are never touched.
    46	\item[\textbf{L1+L2 — stacked rulebook}] — concatenate the L1 rulebook
    47	  and the L2 corrective rules into a single domain rulebook.
    48	  Oracle budget is $K$ per domain (same as L2 alone).
    49	\end{description}
    50	
    51	L1 and L2 capture complementary biases. L1 reads only normal images
    52	and therefore has a \emph{predict-normal bias}: its
    53	\emph{hypothesized anomaly modes} are reasoned from the function and
    54	structure of the ref objects, but never from a real anomaly, so the
    55	rulebook tends to over-accept borderline queries. L2 reads exactly
    56	the dev items Passive v9 is most uncertain about, so its corrective
    57	rules are concentrated on borderline cases and naturally pull in the
    58	opposite direction — adding anomaly modes for dev false-negatives and
    59	tolerances for dev false-positives. The L1+L2 stack therefore tests
    60	whether the two sources combine super-additively.
    61	
    62	\subsection{Method}
    63	\label{ssec:verb_method}
    64	
    65	\paragraph{Rulebook granularity.} We use an adaptive granularity that
    66	follows the dataset schema rather than a tuned hyperparameter: for
    67	domains with more than one class in the \texttt{manifests\_v2}
    68	taxonomy (D1 industrial / $15$ classes, D2 retail / $6$, D3
    69	VisA / $12$, D4 infrastructure / $3$, D5 MVTec-LOCO / $5$, D6
    70	Real3D-AD / $11$) we build a \emph{per-class} rulebook; for the
    71	six single-class domains (D7--D12) we build a single
    72	\emph{per-domain} rulebook. At test time the rulebook for the query's
    73	(domain, class) tuple is injected; the lookup uses the manifest
    74	\texttt{category} field, not an oracle class prediction from the
    75	model.
    76	
    77	\paragraph{L1 protocol.} For each rulebook unit (class or single-class
    78	domain), sample $\min(8,\;|\mathrm{refs}|)$ reference images. A
    79	reflector VLM — the \emph{same} backbone used for the query-time
    80	agent, no stronger model — is asked to emit a strict-JSON rulebook of
    81	the form
    82	\begin{quote}\small\ttfamily
    83	\{"normal\_signature":["...","..."],
    84	  "hypothesized\_anomaly\_modes":["...","..."],\\
    85	 "rules":["if X then lean anomaly","if Y then lean normal"]\}
    86	\end{quote}
    87	The two-field decomposition is a hard requirement of the prompt:
    88	requiring \emph{both} a normal signature and an enumerated anomaly
    89	mode list is our primary counter-measure against L1's predict-normal
    90	bias, and we measure its effect directly
    91	(\S\ref{ssec:verb_bias_diagnostic}).
    92	The reflector sees only ref images, no query and no oracle — so L1
    93	uses zero dev or test exposure.
    94	
    95	\paragraph{L2 protocol.} Run Passive v9 on all $40$ dev items per
    96	domain. Let $\mathcal{U}_K$ be the set of $K{=}10$ dev items with the
    97	smallest $|s{-}0.5|$. For each $i\in\mathcal{U}_K$, reveal its
    98	manifest label $y_i$. A reflector VLM receives
    99	$(x_i,\;\text{agent trajectory}_i,\;y_i,\;\text{L1 rulebook})$ and emits
   100	either (a) an \emph{anomaly-mode extension} when $y_i{=}1$ and the
   101	agent missed it, or (b) a \emph{normal-signature tolerance} when
   102	$y_i{=}0$ and the agent over-flagged. The reflector writes at most
   103	one rule per dev item. The remaining $30$ dev items are held out as
   104	a \textbf{rule-quality holdout} (\S\ref{ssec:verb_holdout}) and are
   105	not touched by the reflector.
   106	
   107	\paragraph{Stacking.} The L1+L2 rulebook is the string concatenation
   108	\texttt{[L1 signature] $\parallel$ [L1 anomaly modes $\cup$ L2
   109	extensions] $\parallel$ [L1 rules $\cup$ L2 corrective rules]}, with
   110	de-duplication at the rule-string level. Rules coming from L2 are
   111	marked ``(corrective)''; we leave a principled conflict resolution
   112	between L1 and L2 predicates to future work.
   113	
   114	\paragraph{Test-time injection.} The rulebook for (domain, class) is
   115	prepended to the turn-$1$ user message of the Passive v9 agent as a
   116	short JSON block tagged \texttt{DOMAIN RULEBOOK}. All other
   117	machinery — mode classifier, refutation protocol, tool set, stopping
   118	criterion — is unchanged. No test labels or test-set items enter the
   119	rulebook pipeline at any stage; the injection is the only test-time
   120	difference from Passive v9.
   121	
   122	\subsection{Experiments}
   123	\label{ssec:verb_exp}
   124	
   125	\paragraph{Setup.} All 12 CrossDomainVAD-11 test domains
   126	($n{=}1{,}418$), Qwen3.5-VL-27B backbone, temperature $0$. Each
   127	variant is run with $3$ seeds; we report the mean across seeds.
   128	Oracle budget for L2 is $K{=}10$ dev items per domain ($120$ total
   129	across the benchmark). All hyperparameters are frozen across domains.
   130	Reflector calls are charged explicitly in Table~\ref{tab:verb_cost}.
   131	
   132	\begin{table}[t]
   133	\centering
   134	\caption{\textbf{Verbalized self-evolution: per-domain AUROC,
   135	Qwen3.5-VL-27B test.} Each cell is the mean of $3$ seeds; oracle
   136	column shows cumulative revealed dev labels. Adaptive granularity:
   137	per-class for D1/D2/D3/D4/D5/D6, per-domain for D7/D8/D9/D10/D11/D12.
   138	All test items are held strictly out of the rulebook-construction
   139	pipeline.}
   140	\label{tab:verb_main}
   141	\small
   142	\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
   143	\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
   144	\toprule
   145	Variant & Oracle / domain & Rulebook source & Macro AUROC & $\Delta$ vs Passive & $P(\Delta{>}0)$ \\
   146	\midrule
   147	Passive v9 (\S\ref{ssec:main_results}) & $0$ & --- & $0.814$ & --- & --- \\
   148	\textbf{+ L1}   (ref-only, self-sup)     & $0$   & refs only           & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
   149	\textbf{+ L2}   (active verbalized)      & $10$  & dev oracle only     & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
   150	\textbf{+ L1+L2} (stacked)               & $10$  & refs + dev oracle   & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
   151	\bottomrule
   152	\end{tabular}
   153	\end{table}
   154	
   155	\paragraph{Bias diagnostic.}
   156	\label{ssec:verb_bias_diagnostic}
   157	For each variant we report the \emph{predict-normal rate} on the
   158	test set (fraction of queries with anomaly score $<0.5$). Our working
   159	hypothesis is that L1-alone will show a higher predict-normal rate
   160	than Passive, L2-alone will show a lower rate, and the L1+L2 stack
   161	will land closest to Passive.
   162	
   163	\begin{table}[h]
   164	\centering
   165	\small
   166	\begin{tabular}{lcc}
   167	\toprule
   168	Variant & Predict-normal rate (test) & $\Delta$ vs Passive \\
   169	\midrule
   170	Passive v9 & \emph{t.b.d.} & $0$ \\
   171	+ L1 & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
   172	+ L2 & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
   173	+ L1+L2 & \emph{t.b.d.} & \emph{t.b.d.} \\
   174	\bottomrule
   175	\end{tabular}
   176	\caption{Bias diagnostic; a signed $\Delta$ measures the predict-normal
   177	drift induced by the rulebook.}
   178	\label{tab:verb_bias}
   179	\end{table}
   180	
   181	\paragraph{Rule-quality holdout.}
   182	\label{ssec:verb_holdout}
   183	The $30$ dev items that the L2 reflector did not see form a held-out
   184	validation set for rulebook quality. We report dev-holdout AUROC of
   185	each variant; this measures rulebook quality without touching the
   186	test split and disentangles ``the rulebook is better'' from ``the
   187	test set happens to benefit.''
   188	
   189	\paragraph{Qualitative rule samples.} For each domain we include
   190	one representative rule from the L1 and L2 rulebooks
   191	(Appendix~\ref{app:verbalized_rules}), with its associated test-item
   192	flip. Example rule classes we expect to see: counting / logical (D5),
   193	symmetry / bilateral (D9 brain MRI), texture anomaly (D7, D8),
   194	scene-level anomaly (D12), missing-component (D1).
   195	
   196	\paragraph{Ablations.}
   197	We report three ablations in Appendix~\ref{app:verbalized_ablations}:
   198	(a) removing the \texttt{hypothesized\_anomaly\_modes} field from the
   199	L1 prompt (tests whether the bias counter-measure works),
   200	(b) per-class vs pooled per-domain rulebook on the multi-class
   201	domains (tests whether class-level granularity is needed),
   202	(c) replacing the dev-oracle in L2 with the same-budget random dev
   203	labels (tests whether uncertainty selection matters).
   204	
   205	\subsection{Limitations}
   206	\label{ssec:verb_limitations}
   207	L1 reads only normal refs and therefore cannot learn truly novel
   208	anomaly classes unless the reflector hallucinates them; L2 is bounded
   209	by the $K$ oracle budget and by the dev set's coverage of the
   210	anomaly manifold; the stacked L1+L2 rulebook does not yet resolve
   211	predicate conflicts between L1 and L2 formally and can in principle
   212	loop if L1 and L2 recommend opposite actions on the same feature.
   213	The rulebook is currently static across test items within a
   214	(domain, class); a dynamic variant that selects rules per-query is
   215	future work.

