\section{Benchmark Specification}
\label{app:benchmark}

Table~\ref{tab:benchmark_detail} provides per-domain details for CrossDomainVAD-11. All splits, item IDs, reference mappings, and domain descriptors will be released as a JSON specification file.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{CrossDomainVAD-11 per-domain specification.}
\label{tab:benchmark_detail}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{cllp{0.40\linewidth}rl}
\toprule
ID & Domain & Source & Anomaly definition & Refs/item & License \\
\midrule
D01 & Industrial & MVTec-AD & Surface defect, damage, contamination & 10 & CC BY-NC-SA \\
D02 & Retail & GoodsAD & Torn label, deformation, missing component & 10 & Research \\
D03 & Infrastructure & SDNET2018 & Cracks, spalling, structural damage & 10 & Public \\
D04 & Dermoscopy & DermaMNIST & Malignant melanoma & 10 & CC BY \\
D05 & Brain MRI & BraTS2021 & Glioma, mass effect, oedema & 10 & CC BY \\
D06 & Liver CT & BMAD-Liver & Focal lesion, tumour, cyst & 10 & Research \\
D07 & Endoscopy & HyperKvasir & Polyp, ulcer, inflammation & 10 & Research \\
D08 & Change Det. & LEVIR-CD+ & Building-level change (construction, demolition) & 1--2 & Research \\
D09 & Road Obstacle & RA21+BDD & Unexpected object on roadway & 2 & Research \\
D10 & Logical & MVTec-LOCO & Wrong count/arrangement of components & 10 & CC BY-NC-SA \\
D11 & VisA & VisA & Surface defect, chips, scratches & 10 & CC BY-NC-SA \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\paragraph{Reference selection.}
References are normal images from the same category/scene, selected deterministically by metadata match then fixed random seed. No reference appears as a query. Patient-level (D04--D07) and event-level (D08) deduplication prevents leakage.

\paragraph{Split protocol.}
Per domain: 20 calibration, 40 dev, 120 test (balanced 50/50, except D08 LEVIR-CD+ with 98 test items). All splits frozen before experiments.

\paragraph{Task-anchored domain descriptors.}
The descriptor $d$ is a single sentence inserted into every VLM prompt. The eleven descriptors used in CrossDomainVAD-11 are:
\begin{itemize}\small\setlength\itemsep{1pt}
    \item \textbf{D01 Industrial (MVTec-AD):} Normal = a manufactured object matching the reference in geometry, texture, and colour. Anomaly = surface defect, damage, contamination, or missing/extra component.
    \item \textbf{D02 Retail (GoodsAD):} Normal = a packaged supermarket product matching the reference in label, shape, and surface. Anomaly = torn label, deformation, missing component, or printing defect.
    \item \textbf{D03 Infrastructure (SDNET2018):} Normal = intact concrete surface. Anomaly = crack, spalling, or structural damage.
    \item \textbf{D04 Dermoscopy (DermaMNIST):} Normal = benign melanocytic skin lesion. Anomaly = malignant melanoma.
    \item \textbf{D05 Brain MRI (BraTS2021):} Normal = healthy brain MRI slice. Anomaly = glioma, mass effect, or peritumoural oedema.
    \item \textbf{D06 Liver CT (BMAD-Liver):} Normal = healthy liver CT slice. Anomaly = focal lesion, tumour, or cyst.
    \item \textbf{D07 GI Endoscopy (HyperKvasir):} Normal = clean gastrointestinal mucosa. Anomaly = polyp, ulcer, or inflammation.
    \item \textbf{D08 Change Detection (LEVIR-CD+):} Normal = no building change between reference and query. Anomaly = building-level change including new construction, demolition, or expansion. \emph{Planned urbanisation IS the anomaly}.
    \item \textbf{D09 Road Obstacle (RA21+BDD):} Normal = empty driveable roadway (possibly with expected vehicles and markings). Anomaly = unexpected object on the roadway.
    \item \textbf{D10 Logical (MVTec-LOCO):} Normal = correct count and spatial arrangement of components. Anomaly = wrong count, wrong position, or wrong component type.
    \item \textbf{D11 VisA (VisA):} Normal = a manufactured object matching the reference. Anomaly = surface defect, chips, scratches, or missing component.
\end{itemize}

A corresponding generic baseline descriptor used in Finding~1 reads simply: ``\emph{Identify whether the query image is anomalous relative to the reference(s).}''

\section{Score Extraction Rules}
\label{app:scoring}

All VLM variants output structured JSON. The anomaly score $s \in [0, 1]$:

\paragraph{v0 Direct.}
Output: \texttt{\{image\_label, confidence, anomaly\_type\}}.
$s = c$ if label $=$ ``anomalous'', $s = 1{-}c$ if ``normal'', where $c$ is reported confidence. Parse failure rate: $<1\%$ across all backbones.

\paragraph{AnomalyClaw interpret.}
Same JSON schema and scoring. Final score: interpret output on escalated items, $s_0$ on committed items.

\paragraph{Route B.}
$s = 0.3 \cdot s_0 + 0.7 \cdot \sigma(\text{expert\_score})$, where $\sigma$ normalises relative to calibration median.

\section{Failed agent variants}
\label{app:ablation}

Table~\ref{tab:ablation_appendix} reports the six alternative agent designs we tested on GPT-5.4 calibration. All were fixed and tuned on the same 220-item calibration split as AnomalyClaw. Failure analysis per variant:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Ablation of agent variants (GPT-5.4, calibration macro AUROC). All use the same descriptor-enhanced v0 as the base.}
\label{tab:ablation_appendix}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
   330	The descriptor router maps each domain family to a default strategy. Rules were frozen once from the benchmark descriptor before any test query; no calibration AUROC was consulted.
   331	
   332	\begin{table}[h]
   333	\centering
   334	\caption{Descriptor router rules.}
   335	\label{tab:router_rules}
   336	\small
   337	\begin{tabular}{llp{7cm}}
   338	\toprule
   339	Family & Strategy & Reasoning \\
   340	\midrule
   341	industrial / retail / industrial\_visa & fusion & texture-dominant few-shot; expert is reliably discriminative, VLM calibrates \\
   342	infrastructure & fusion & concrete cracks flagged by patch expert; descriptor narrows false positives \\
   343	logical (D9 MVTec-LOCO) & fusion & component structure visible to patch expert; VLM rules out semantic look-alikes \\
   344	dermoscopy (D4) & fusion & patch expert catches localised lesions; VLM rules out imaging artefacts \\
   345	medical\_mixed / liver\_ct / brain\_mri & fusion & SubspaceAD complements VLM on non-trivial intensity shifts \\
   346	gi\_endoscopy (D7, D5d) & direct & VLM-strong: mucosa colour/shape semantics beyond generic feature distance \\
   347	remote-sensing change (D6 LEVIR) & direct & change semantics require descriptor-level task anchoring \\
   348	road (D8, D9 RA21+BDD) & direct & semantic obstacle recognition dominates; expert is a distractor \\
   349	\bottomrule
   350	\end{tabular}
   351	\end{table}
   352	
   353	\section{Tool contracts}
   354	\label{app:tools}
   355	
   356	Each tool has a small Python contract. Below are the full signatures; the implementations (Python/NumPy) are in \texttt{refine-logs/anomaclaw\_v2/registry.py}.
   357	
   358	\begin{itemize}
   359	    \item \texttt{domain\_descriptor(domain\_code) $\to$ str}: returns the task-anchored descriptor. Zero cost, no side effect.
   360	    \item \texttt{reference\_retriever(query, refs, k=3) $\to$ list[int]}: cosine similarity of DINOv2-CLS tokens; returns top-$k$ reference indices.
   361	    \item \texttt{hotspot\_cropper(query, patches, k=5, pad=0.15) $\to$ bbox}: takes the top-$k$ expert patches, computes a padded bounding box in image coordinates, returns a crop for the VLM.
   362	    \item \texttt{component\_counter(hotspot\_map, threshold=0.5) $\to$ (n, stats)}: connected-component labelling with skimage; returns component count and per-component area/centroid.
   363	    \item \texttt{knowledge\_lookup(domain\_code) $\to$ list[str]}: returns a fixed, per-domain keyword list (length $\le 6$; written once during benchmark setup).
   364	\end{itemize}
   365	

