benchmark/results/agent_v3/agent_test_D9.json:1379:        "reasoning": "The query matches the general layout of two transparent connectors linked by a single yellow wire, but the connector blocks do not appear to match the normal 3-position configuration. In the references, each side consistently shows three stacked orange actuator sections, while in the query both connectors appear to show only two orange sections. That suggests a different connector type or incomplete assembly, which is a logical mismatch from the normal pattern. Wire color and slight curvature are normal variations, so the likely anomaly is the connector configuration itself."
benchmark/results/agent_v3/agent_test_D9.json:3113:        "reasoning": "The query matches the normal organizer-case pattern: it is the same transparent compartment case viewed from above, contains only yellow push pins, and occupied compartments each appear to contain a single loose pin. Empty compartments are present and acceptable, and the varied pin orientations are consistent with the normal references. I do not see extra components, missing structural case elements, or any compartment with multiple stacked pins."
benchmark/results/agent_v3/agent_test_D9.json:3147:        "reasoning": "The query matches the normal product structure: it is the same transparent compartment organizer viewed from above, and all visible contents are yellow/orange push pins with metal needles. Each compartment appears to contain at most one loose push pin, with no foreign objects, obvious damage, stacking, or missing case structure. Pin orientations vary naturally, which is consistent with the references. Although this query appears more fully populated than the examples, that does not by itself indicate a logical assembly defect."
benchmark/results/agent_v3/agent_test_D9.json:3997:        "reasoning": "The query image matches the normal pattern: it shows the same transparent compartment organizer viewed from above, containing only yellow/orange push pins. Occupancy varies by compartment, which is allowed, and the visible pins appear to be single loose pins rather than stacked multiples. Empty compartments and varied orientations are consistent with the normal references. I do not see any foreign object, missing structural component of the case, or logical packing error."
